-helper class->gives empty implementation for all methods of an interface
-so we dont have to implemrnt every method of interface

Why do we need an Adapter Class?
Problem without adapter class:

If an interface has many methods, then:
interface Listener {
    void click();
    void doubleClick();
    void mouseEnter();
    void mouseExit();
}
If you want only click(), you still must write all methods 😖

Solution:
 -Adapter class
class ListenerAdapter implements Listener {
    public void click() {}
    public void doubleClick() {}
    public void mouseEnter() {}
    public void mouseExit() {}
}

Now you extend the adapter 👇
class MyApp extends ListenerAdapter {
    public void click() {
        System.out.println("Click handled");
    }
}
✔ You override only what you need
use:
To reduce code
To avoid implementing unwanted methods
To make code clean and simple
To provide default implementations for interface method
-“Adapter class is used when an interface has many methods but we need only a few. It provides
empty implementations so we can override only required methods.”

interface KeyboardListener {
    void keyPressed();
    void keyReleased();
    void keyTyped();
}

// Adapter class
class KeyboardAdapter implements KeyboardListener {
    public void keyPressed() {}
    public void keyReleased() {}
    public void keyTyped() {}
}

// Your class (use only one method)
class MyKeyboard extends KeyboardAdapter {
    public void keyPressed() {
        System.out.println("Key is pressed");
    }
}

// Test class
public class TestKeyboard {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MyKeyboard k = new MyKeyboard();
        k.keyPressed();
    }
}✅ Output
 Key is pressed


