1. class implements interface:
-When a class implements an interface, it must override ALL methods of the interface.

interface Vehicle {
    void start();
    void stop();
}

class Bike implements Vehicle {
    public void start() {
        System.out.println("Bike started");
    }

    public void stop() {
        System.out.println("Bike stopped");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Vehicle v = new Bike();
        v.start();
        v.stop();
    }
}
-Interface contains only abstract methods (before Java 8).
-Class must implement all interface methods.
-If not, class must be abstract.
-Multiple interfaces can be implemented.
-Used to achieve 100% abstraction & multiple inheritance.


✅ 2. Class extends Abstract Class
📌 Rule

When a class extends an abstract class, it must override only
abstract methods, not concrete ones.

🔹 Working Program
abstract class Bank {
    abstract void rateOfInterest();

    void display() {
        System.out.println("Bank details");
    }
}

class SBI extends Bank {
    void rateOfInterest() {
        System.out.println("ROI is 10%");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Bank b = new SBI();
        b.rateOfInterest();
        b.display();
    }
}

🎤 5 Interview Points

Abstract class can have abstract + concrete methods.
Child class must override only abstract methods.
Can have constructors & instance variables.
Supports partial abstraction.
A class can extend only one abstract class.

✅ 3. Class extends Adapter Class
📌 Rule

Adapter class implements interface with empty methods,
 so child overrides only required ones.

🔹 Working Program
interface MouseListener {
    void click();
    void press();
    void release();
}

abstract class MouseAdapter implements MouseListener {
    public void click() {}
    public void press() {}
    public void release() {}
}

class MyMouse extends MouseAdapter {
    public void click() {
        System.out.println("Mouse clicked");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MouseListener m = new MyMouse();
        m.click();
    }
}

🎤 5 Interview Points

Adapter class reduces boilerplate code.
Child class overrides only needed methods.
Adapter class is usually abstract
Used when interface has many methods.
Common in event handling (AWT/Swing).

✅ 4. Class extends Normal Class
📌 Rule

Child class overrides methods only if behavior change is needed.

🔹 Working Program
class Parent {
    void show() {
        System.out.println("Parent show");
    }
}

class Child extends Parent {
    void show() {
        System.out.println("Child show");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Parent p = new Child();
        p.show();
    }
}

🎤 5 Interview Points

Normal class has only concrete methods.
Overriding is optional, not mandatory.
Used for runtime polymorphism.
Method signature must be same.
Access level cannot be reduced.


